<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="guanosine nucleotides degradation II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6606" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The distinction between nucleoside degradation and salvage is not always straight forward. A general rule is that degradation pathways start with the nucleotide forms and convert them to simpler forms, eventually leading to complete mineralization, while salvage pathways start with either the nucleoside or the free base form, and convert those to the nucleotide forms. Nucleotide recycling is achieved by a combination of both types - a nucleotide is partially degraded via a degradation pathway, but the products are shuttled into a salvage pathway rather then towrds complete mineralization. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In most plants, purine nucleotides are completely degraded to |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| and |FRAME: AMMONIA| via the ureides |FRAME: S-ALLANTOIN| and |FRAME: ALLANTOATE|. This degradation proceeds via the intermediates |FRAME: XANTHINE| and |FRAME: URATE|. The initial step of GMP degradation in this pathway is the conversion of GMP to |FRAME: GUANOSINE|, which can be degraded to |FRAME: XANTHINE| via |FRAME: GUANINE|. The enzyme that converts |FRAME: GUANOSINE| to |FRAME: GUANINE|, guanosine deaminase, has been reported in fungi |CITS: [7551735]| and green plants |CITS: [GURANOWSKI82]|. A different route from |FRAME: GUANOSINE| to |FRAME: XANTHINE| is described in |FRAME: PWY-6607|. |FRAME: XANTHINE| is converted to |FRAME:URATE| by xanthine dehydrogenase |CITS: [14726515]|, and the latter is converted to |FRAME: S-ALLANTOIN| as described in |FRAME: PWY-5691|. This plant/fungal pathway is very similar to that found in mammals. However, mammals do not have the enzyme |FRAME: MONOMER-11783| (EC 3.5.4.15) and instead use |FRAME: CPLX-6181| (EC 2.4.2.1) |CITS: [14725335]| (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-6608|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="xue" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
